HSRP Failure Test

The lab can be used to demonstrate gateway redundancy and HSRP failover.

Step 1 — Verify R2 is Active
show standby brief

Expected state:

R2: Active
R3: Standby
Step 2 — Verify R3 is Standby
show standby brief

R3 should report the Standby state for the HSRP groups.

Step 3 — Simulate an R2 Failure

Shut down the appropriate R2 VLAN subinterface or interface being used for the test.

Example:

R2(config)#interface GigabitEthernet1/0.10
R2(config-subif)#shutdown
Step 4 — Verify R3 Becomes Active

On R3:

show standby brief

R3 should transition to the Active state for the affected HSRP group.

Step 5 — Restore R2
R2(config)#interface GigabitEthernet1/0.10
R2(config-subif)#no shutdown
Step 6 — Verify Preemption

Because R2 has the higher priority and preemption is enabled, R2 should regain the Active role after recovering.

show standby brief

Expected result:

R2: Active
R3: Standby
Design Summary

HSRP is implemented across VLANs 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, and 60 to provide redundant default gateway services.

R2 is configured with a priority of 110 and acts as the preferred Active router. R3 is configured with a priority of 100 and operates as the Standby router.

Each VLAN uses an HSRP Virtual IP address ending in .1, while R2 and R3 use .2 and .3 respectively.

HSRP version 2 is used across all VLANs, and preemption is enabled so that R2 can reclaim the Active role after recovering from a failure.

The design provides first-hop redundancy while allowing both R2 and R3 to participate in routing and provide gateway services for the VLANs.
